# Local finance application

This application supports local password authentication with no external API
keys. Google OAuth, Stripe and Resend are optional. Development mail appears in
the application log (or Mailpit with Docker).

## Create and run

Install the Go version required by go.mod, Node.js and npm, and the Vukem CLI.
To generate this application with its optional initial data:

```sh
vukem new money --example finance --seed
cd money
npm install
npm run build:emails
vukem serve
```

Open http://localhost:3000. The CLI generates APP_KEY and the initial seed runs
migrations. Without `--seed`, generation creates no database records; run the
following when ready:

```sh
vukem migrate
vukem db:seed FinanceAdminSeeder FinanceDemoSeeder
```

The development administrator email is `admin@example.test`. The seed generates
a random password and reports a private `storage/finance-admin-*.txt` file;
read that file locally to sign in at `/login`, then visit `/dashboard` or
`/admin`. Keep storage ignored by Git. The file contains local credentials;
do not share it or publish it as an artifact. You can instead set your own
password before the first seed in `.env`:

```dotenv
APP_ENV=development
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.test
FINANCE_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.test
FINANCE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=replace-with-your-own-random-password-at-least-16-characters
MAIL_MAILER=log
```

Leave FINANCE_ADMIN_PASSWORD empty to generate it securely. APP_KEY must remain
the unique generated application key. ADMIN_EMAILS must include the seed email.
Existing users are never assigned a new password or promoted by the seed.
Repeated seeds leave existing users and demo data unchanged, including edits.
The demo uses USD, integer cents, and November 2026 ISO dates; choose November
2026 to view those transactions. An existing workspace membership makes the demo
seed a no-op. Both finance seeders refuse production, including with `--force`.
Use the regular registration and verification flow for production accounts.

## Optional Google sign-in

Create a Google OAuth web application and add the exact redirect URI:
`http://localhost:3000/auth/google/callback`. Set these values in `.env`:

```dotenv
SOCIAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-google-client-id
SOCIAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-client-secret
```

Restart the application. The sign-in button opens `/auth/google/redirect`.
Neither credential is needed for password login; both must be supplied together
for Google. Google must return a verified email. Existing verified email accounts
link to that provider identity, and local two-factor authentication still applies.
For deployment set APP_URL to the public HTTPS origin and register
`https://your-host/auth/google/callback` with Google. Multiple application
instances must share their OAuth state cache (database or Redis).

## Docker

Every finance app includes `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore` and `compose.yaml`.
The image needs the JavaScript lockfile from `npm install` (or the package
manager you generated with). Compose publishes the app on 127.0.0.1:3000,
keeps storage in the `storage` volume, and starts Mailpit at
http://localhost:8025. PostgreSQL is defined for an optional switch; the
default database is the SQLite file in that storage volume.

Run these commands in the generated project. Keep the generated `.env` and
APP_KEY; use a separate ignored `app.env` for runtime configuration:

```sh
npm install
npm run build:emails
cp .env app.env
chmod 600 app.env
```

Edit `app.env` to set APP_ENV=development, APP_URL=http://localhost:3000,
HOST=0.0.0.0, PORT=3000, MAIL_MAILER=log, ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.test,
DATABASE_URL=file:storage/database.sqlite, and optionally FINANCE_ADMIN_EMAIL and
FINANCE_ADMIN_PASSWORD. Add `app.env` to `.gitignore` and `.dockerignore` before
building; secrets are passed only at runtime. The following uses the persistent
storage volume and the same environment for migration, seeding and serving:

```sh
docker build -t money-local .
docker volume create money-storage
docker run --rm --env-file app.env -v money-storage:/app/storage money-local migrate
docker run --rm --env-file app.env -v money-storage:/app/storage money-local db:seed FinanceAdminSeeder FinanceDemoSeeder
```

The image uses `/app/app` as its entrypoint. Read the generated credential
file by overriding that entrypoint, then start the server:

```sh
docker run --rm --entrypoint /bin/sh -v money-storage:/app/storage money-local -c 'cat storage/finance-admin-*.txt'
docker run --rm --name money-local -p 127.0.0.1:3000:3000 --env-file app.env -v money-storage:/app/storage money-local
```

Open http://localhost:3000. `docker compose up --build` is also available when
compose.yaml was generated: it reads `.env`, migrates automatically and provides
Mailpit at http://localhost:8025. Seed that stack with
`docker compose exec app /app/app db:seed FinanceAdminSeeder FinanceDemoSeeder`.
Compose does not seed automatically. Never reuse local administrator credentials
or APP_ENV=development for production.
